`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Growing up, I switched between German and Russian constantly, with my parents, between rooms, sometimes mid-sentence. It felt completely natural. As an adult, I became more curious about my parents' immigration story and the broader question of how language and identity travel across borders together. That curiosity sent me down a rabbit hole into multilingualism research: specifically, what actually ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"triggers"})," a switch? Is it the topic? The person you're talking to? A word that simply doesn't exist in the other language?"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["I came across the ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"http://bangortalk.org.uk/speakers.php?c=miami",children:"Miami Bangor Corpus"}),", a collection of naturalistic bilingual conversations recorded in Miami. I focused on the Miami subset, Spanish-English speakers, partly because Spanish-English code-switching is one of the most studied contact varieties in the world, and partly because the dataset is genuinely accessible and well-annotated. It made for a good anchor to ask a question I'd been sitting with for years."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Interactive Corpus Explorer"}),`
`,e.jsx("iframe",{src:"/code-switching-visualisation.html",style:{width:"100%",height:"620px",border:"none"},title:"Miami Corpus — Code-Switch Viewer"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Overview"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The goal was to identify the linguistic and contextual patterns that predict a language switch in natural conversation. When does a Spanish-English speaker reach for the other language — and can a model learn to anticipate that?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"I used Python to process the corpus transcripts, extracting turn-level features: word frequency, part of speech, speaker identity, conversational context. From there, I trained classifiers (sklearn for baselines, pytorch for sequence models) to predict switch points and explored what the models weighted most heavily."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Detail"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"The corpus"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(t.a,{href:"http://bangortalk.org.uk/speakers.php?c=miami",children:"Miami Bangor Corpus"})," contains 56 naturalistic conversations recorded in Miami with Spanish-English bilinguals — around 52,000 utterances and 212,000 tokens across four speaker families (Herring, Maria, Sastre, Zeledon). Crucially, the corpus annotates language choice at multiple levels of granularity. Whole utterances can be flagged as Spanish-headed (",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[- spa]"}),"), individual tokens within an utterance can be tagged with their language (",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"@s:spa"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"@s:eng"}),"), and morphologically hybrid words — like ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"printear"})," (English ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"print"})," + Spanish -ear infinitive) or ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"faxeando"})," (English ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"fax"})," + Spanish gerund) — get their own compound tags. Every utterance also has an auto-generated part-of-speech gloss tier, giving token-level morphosyntactic features."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["One thing that stands out immediately: switching is not evenly distributed. Some conversations are almost entirely English; others flip languages on nearly every other turn. Zeledon8 and Sastre3, for instance, are Spanish-headed on around 37–40% of turns. This inter-speaker variability turned out to be important for modelling — it's not just ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"what"})," you're talking about that predicts a switch, it's ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"who"})," you are."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Two tasks"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"I framed prediction as two separate classification problems:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Token-level:"})," given a token, does the next token switch language? This is a highly localised prediction — it asks whether any individual word is a switch boundary."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Utterance-level:"})," given the last token of an utterance, does the next utterance switch language? This is coarser but arguably more linguistically meaningful — it captures the full conversational turn as the unit of analysis."]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Both are heavily imbalanced. Token-level switches make up only 1.2% of the test set (867 switches out of 56,168 tokens). Utterance-level is more tractable at about 16% positive (1,897 out of 11,622 utterances). Train/test splits were done by conversation — all tokens from a given conversation go entirely to one side — to avoid data leakage across turns from the same speaker pair."}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:e.jsx(t.img,{src:"/cs-class-balance.png",alt:"Class distribution across both test sets"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.h3,{children:"Features"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Ten features went into the baseline classifiers:"}),`
