`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The dominance of language-tag features over structural ones is striking. The model is not primarily learning ",e.jsx(t.em,{children:"where"})," in an utterance switches happen — it is learning the local language context around the switch point. A token in Spanish inside an English-base utterance is highly predictive of a switch back to English on the very next token, regardless of whether that token is at the start, middle, or end of the utterance. POS tag has a meaningful contribution too — certain categories (conjunctions, discourse markers, proper nouns) are more likely to occur at switch boundaries."]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"What drops out as nearly irrelevant: whether the token is the first or last in the utterance, and utterance length. The structural position of a token carries almost no information once you condition on the surrounding language context."}),`
